{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316006} {:answer/id #uuid "d749d0ae-a22d-5ed1-be30-0b1f60b7b732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e38dfcb-edf6-509c-b949-c49211760623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316006} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2d45990-077c-5ce4-90c0-dbfc23fdd7cd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad2f00c-b8bd-52f8-8259-63fb31e76519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315457} {:answer/id #uuid "4a00f21f-5599-5f72-8b88-407db5f68046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315542} {:answer/id #uuid "54a4284d-878d-5837-bf31-4feba40875d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315604} {:answer/id #uuid "d22fec79-5cae-53ba-a6d9-86446d3ff8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22fec79-5cae-53ba-a6d9-86446d3ff8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316295} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa42d7e4-a254-55bd-8ac0-ef1e79e9d268" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7403504-1eb6-58c7-80a7-8e8d0a72b009" :question/math-academy-id "q-343380" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-2,2).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $4$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}∼-2x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-2t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & ∼-2x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d164cd9-05f8-521d-a1c1-4640e45ea11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430675} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430676} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bb3694-2f5f-53a7-8495-088462a1c3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430677} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8c507d-8e09-52ff-853e-45a9bff03a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430676} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19ae7dd-5f1e-5b74-9ed5-f098fbd5c4d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430672} {:answer/id #uuid "426d62eb-dc4e-5375-bd07-e202606e3cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430673} {:answer/id #uuid "61b492ca-50cf-52f7-8792-35c237dc1fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430674} {:answer/id #uuid "d8196464-3a2a-5b97-9d21-a30214f0b784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430672} :answer-field/id #uuid "223bd29e-2a90-5e8f-a7b8-ad5a6d0bbcc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ad9a596-b8c8-5727-be8e-f1a040ae71ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-343382" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=2\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))+x(-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 4\\pi B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224864 :question/id #uuid "039259c6-f7c8-5017-847d-6d1d27f87d2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-19883" :question/problem "The expression $60x^2 + 180x$ is equivalent to $rx(3x + 9)$, where $r$ is a constant. What is the value of $r$?" :question/worked-solution "We begin with the expression $60x^2 + 180x$ and compare it to the form $rx(3x + 9)$.\n\nFirst, we factor $x$ from both terms in the original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x^{2} + 180x &= x(60x + 180)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out the greatest common factor from the binomial $60x + 180$. The GCF of $60$ and $180$ is $60$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(60x + 180) &= x(60)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 60x(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told the expression is written as $rx(3x + 9)$. Notice that\n\n$$\n3x + 9 = 3(x + 3)\n$$\n\nso we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x(x + 3) &= (3 \\cdot \\frac{60}{3})x(x + 3) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{60}{3}\\right) \\cdot x \\cdot 3(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 20x(3x + 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $r$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{20}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224915 :question/id #uuid "4e1ac044-ddf7-5fac-bc49-fe3a6ac10928" :question/math-academy-id "e-20117" :question/problem "Two variables, $h$ and $j$, are related such that for each increase of $1$ in the value of $h$, the value of $j$ is multiplied by $\\dfrac{3}{2}$. When $h = 0$, $j = 64$. Which equation represents this relationship?" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the value of $j$ is multiplied by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nfor each increase of $1$ in $h$. That means the variable $j$ grows exponentially with base\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h$ and $j$ are related by the expression\n\n$$\nj = a \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^h\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $j = 64$ when $h = 0$. Hence, we can determine the value of $a$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nj &= a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{h} \\\\\n64 &= a \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{0} \\\\\n64 &= a \\cdot 1 \\\\\na &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nj = 64 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^h\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224918 :question/id #uuid "ec39c33f-7c10-526a-a7ef-6a09826afb2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-20129" :question/problem "A machine was purchased in $2015$ for $\\[math]32{,}000$. Its value halves every $6$ years. In which year will the value reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that a machine was purchased in $2015$ for $\\[math]32{,}000$, and its value halves every $6$ years. We want to find the year when the value will reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$.\n\nThe formula for exponential decay, in terms of a halving period $d$, is\n\n$$\ny = a \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/d}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=32{,}000$ is the initial value in $2015$.\n- The value halves every $6$ years. So, the halving period is $d=6$.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after $2015$.\n\nTherefore, the expression for the value $y$ after $t$ years is\n\n$$\ny = 32{,}000 \\cdot \\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $t$ when\n\n$$\ny = 2{,}000\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 2{,}000$ in the last equation and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,000 &= 32,000 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{2,000}{32,000} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{16} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= \\frac{t}{6} \\\\\nt &= 4 \\cdot 6 \\\\\nt &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $24$ years for the value to reach $\\[math]2{,}000$.\n\nFinally, to find the year, we add this to the initial year $2015{:}$\n\n$$\n2015 + 24 = 2039\n$$\n\nSo, the value will reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$ in the year ${2039}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186224921 :question/id #uuid "6ac4a2b8-d1f6-5e95-890e-6af40e659151" :question/math-academy-id "e-20134" :question/problem "An exponential function $D$ is defined by $D(x) = 400 \\cdot b^x$, where $b$ is a positive constant. If $D(2) = 100$, what is the value of $D(5)$?" :question/worked-solution "We are told that\n\n$$\nD(x) = 400 \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nand that $D(2) = 100$.\n\nSubstituting $x = 2$ into the function $D$ and solve for $b$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(2) &= 400 \\cdot b^{2} \\\\\n100 &= 400 \\cdot b^{2} \\\\\nb^{2} &= \\frac{100}{400} \\\\\nb^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nb &= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $b$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nb = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the function to calculate $D(5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(5) &= 400 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{5} \\\\\n&= 400 \\cdot \\frac{1}{32} \\\\\n&= 12.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225012 :question/id #uuid "20b8be63-65cc-5636-a28a-f713f5289468" :question/math-academy-id "e-21232" :question/problem "The equation below relates the distinct positive real numbers $a$, $b$, and $z{:}$\n$\\dfrac{36a}{6b} = 12\\sqrt{z + 10}$.\nExpress $z$ in terms of $a$ and $b$." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can simplify the fraction left-hand side since $36$ and $6$ have a common factor of $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36a}{6b} = \\dfrac{6a}{b}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6a}{b} = 12\\sqrt{z + 10}\n$$\n\nTo express our equation in terms of $z$, we first isolate the square root by dividing both sides by $12{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{6 a}{b \\cdot  12}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{12 \\sqrt{z + 10}}{12}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{6 a}{b \\cdot  12}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{12} \\sqrt{z + 10}}{\\cancel{12}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{2 b}} & \\displaystyle  = \\sqrt{z + 10}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{2b}\\right)^2 = z + 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{a}{2b}\\right)^{2} &= z + 10 \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{a}{2b}\\right)^{2} - 10 &= z + 10 - 10 \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{a}{2b}\\right)^{2} - 10 &= z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nz = \\left(\\dfrac{a}{2b}\\right)^2 - 10\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225020 :question/id #uuid "479869ce-10a1-5b7f-905f-8c2d8b219a76" :question/math-academy-id "e-21242" :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection." :question/worked-solution "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + (-\\frac{4}{5} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225029 :question/id #uuid "e47b9965-8c51-5789-8209-cbb12c0e542b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21252" :question/problem "The function\n$P(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}$\nmodels the population of bacteria in a sealed container $t$ weeks after a chemical is introduced. According to the model, what percentage is the population predicted to decrease by every $2$ days? Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Explanation:\n\nWe are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nP(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the population $t$ weeks after the chemical is introduced. We want to rewrite this model in terms of days instead of weeks.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $7$ days in a week, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{d}{7}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $7$ (not multiply) because we want $d=7$ days to correspond to $t=1$ week.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $p(d)$ for the population as a function of time in days $d$ after the chemical is introduced:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(d) = P\\left(\\frac{d}{7}\\right) &= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4) \\cdot (d/7)} \\\\\n&= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{3d/28}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage decrease over $2$ days. Inspecting the model in terms of days $p(d)$, we see that the decay factor over $d$ days is\n$(0.95)^{3d/28}$.\n\nSo, the decay factor over $2$ days is\n\n$$\n(0.95)^{3\\cdot 2/28} = (0.95)^{3/14} \\approx 0.989\\,069\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to a decrease of approximately\n\n$$\n1 - 0.989\\,069 = 0.010\\,931 \\approx 1.09\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the population is predicted to decrease by $1.09\\%$ every $2$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225031 :question/id #uuid "068a7ba4-70c4-5bbb-bd7a-64fe3f036a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-21254" :question/problem "The function is defined by $f(x) = -a^{x} + d$, where $a$ and $d$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $d$ is $\\dfrac{21}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225032 :question/id #uuid "2cbdbc3c-9016-53b4-8414-42e451321415" :question/math-academy-id "e-21255" :question/problem "The function\n$C(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}$\nmodels the number of data packets processed, in thousands, by a server $t$ minutes after a throughput upgrade. According to the model, in how many seconds is the number of packets expected to increase by $5\\%$?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nC(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the number of data packets processed, in thousands, $t$ minutes after the upgrade. We want to rewrite this model in terms of seconds rather than minutes.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{s}{60}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $60$ (not multiply) because we want $s=60$ seconds to correspond to $t=1$ minute.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $c(s)$ for the number of packets as a function of time in seconds $s$ after the upgrade:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(s) = C\\left(\\frac{s}{60}\\right) &= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2) \\cdot (s/60)} \\\\\n&= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{s/24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe base $1.05$ means that the number of packets grows by $5\\%$ each time the exponent increases by $1$, i.e., once every growth period.\n\nThe exponent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{24}\n$$\n\nshows that for every increase in $s=24$ seconds, the exponent increases by $1$.\n\nTherefore, the number of packets is expected to increase by $5\\%$ every ${24}$ seconds."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225045 :question/id #uuid "bf86e910-6c38-5a48-81a1-34af8ab1d59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21269" :question/problem "The expression $4x - g$ is a factor of $16x^{2} + 12x - 7g$. Which of the following could be the value of $g$?\n\n1. $g=0$ or $g=4$\n2. $g=4$ only\n3. $g=-1$ or $g=0$\n4. $g=0$ only" :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\n\nNow, since $4x - g$ is a factor of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)=16x^{2} + 12x - 7g\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{g}{4}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{g}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the expression for $p(x)$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{g}{4}\\right) &= 16\\left(\\frac{g}{4}\\right)^{2} + 12\\left(\\frac{g}{4}\\right) - 7g \\\\\n&= g^{2} + 3g - 7g \\\\\n&= g^{2} - 4g \\\\\n&= g(g - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the zero product rule, we conclude that $g(g - 4)=0$ if\n\n$$\ng=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad g-4=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $g=0$ or $g=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225046 :question/id #uuid "82246586-84f6-5a40-a17e-9144d2ef21f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21270" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions has a factor of $2x - 5m$, where $m$ is a **negative integer** constant?\n\n1. $4x^{2} + 2x + 18m$\n2. $4x^{2} + 2x + 10m$\n3. $4x^{2} + 2x + 20m$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225068 :question/id #uuid "e9064009-9634-5b8d-a398-58a6db00179a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21303" :question/problem "A rocket is launched upward and after each stage separation it reaches $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ the height it reached in the previous stage. The rocket initially reaches a height of $64.0 \\, \\text{m}$.\n\nWhat is the maximum height the rocket reaches above the ground between the first and second stage separations?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that a rocket is launched upward to an initial height of $64.0\\,\\text{m}$. After each stage separation, it reaches\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthe height it reached on the previous stage.\n\nThis scenario represents exponential decay, where the height is multiplied by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nafter each stage. The general form for this type of relationship can be expressed as\n\n$$\nH(n) = a \\cdot b^n\n$$\n\nwhere $H(n)$ is the height after $n$ stages, $a$ is the initial height, and $b$ is the decay factor.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=64.0$ is the initial height of the rocket.\n- $b = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is the decay factor.\n- $n$ represents the number of stage separations.\n\nSo, the expression for $H(n)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(n) &= 64.0 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are looking for the height between the first and second stage separations. This corresponds to the height after the first separation. Hence, we need to calculate $H(1)$.\n\nSubstitute $n=1$ into the formula for $H(n)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH(1) &= 64.0 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{1} \\\\\n&= 64.0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{64.0}{2} \\\\\n&= 32.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum height the rocket reaches above the ground between the first and second stage separations is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{32.0}\\,\\text{m}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225069 :question/id #uuid "11ec5b75-416b-52b7-a731-8338a3cafaad" :question/math-academy-id "e-21304" :question/problem "In a science experiment, Priya heated a metal rod and then left it to cool in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. At $3{:}00$ p.m., the rod’s temperature was $200^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $170^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $146.5^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. The rod continued to cool over time.\n\nWrite the function that best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the rod $t$ minutes after it was removed from the heat." :question/worked-solution "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot (\\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5})^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225074 :question/id #uuid "8deefa88-ce6b-5a15-98be-fdac3bb3788b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21316" :question/problem "Let $z(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid$. For which values of $k$ does $z(k) = 5k$?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$25 - 5k|= 5k; 25|= 10k; k|= \\frac{5}{2}$.\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$25 - 5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)|= 25 - \\frac{25}{2}; = \\frac{25}{2}; \\ge 0✓$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$-25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0$,\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225086 :question/id #uuid "7b2a2b9c-8780-5800-a673-551f45918bc2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21380" :question/problem "The expression $5a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{9}b^2$ is equivalent to $5(a - cb)(a + cb)$, where $c$ is a positive constant. What is the value of $c$?" :question/worked-solution "Since our equivalent expression has a constant factor $5$, we start by factoring $5$ out of our original expression:\n\n$$\n5a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{9}b^2 = 5\\left(a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{45}b^2\\right)\n$$\n\nTo write our bracketed expression as a difference of squares, we must write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45}\n$$\n\nas a square. Applying the exponent rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{45}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nHence, we apply our formula for factoring a difference of squares:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\n$$\n\nWith $a=a$ and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nthis yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(a^{2} - \\frac{4}{45}b^{2}) &= 5(a^{2} - \\left(\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}b^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(a^{2} - \\left(\\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(a - \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}})(a + \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\\n&= 5(a - \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b)(a + \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cffa90c-f3e3-51f7-98a4-d34a380030ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = 1, 200 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{t/12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222802} {:answer/id #uuid "9eca8282-6812-573b-84cd-2a4144e2db6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = 1, 200 \\cdot (3t)^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222803} {:answer/id #uuid "d95e6026-077b-5df7-915f-71af3b8288bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = 1, 200 \\cdot 12^{t/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222804} {:answer/id #uuid "ff152d92-8b38-5ea1-b04b-729241548c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = 1, 200 \\cdot 3^{t/12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222805} {:answer/id #uuid "25274599-2706-5d71-894e-a7f2c3b43ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = 1, 200 \\cdot (1.5)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222806}] :answer-field/id #uuid "45468e56-9c88-58bd-aeb0-93d3cac9a150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222801}] :question/id #uuid "fdef1933-5e1c-5e7f-9da8-f7e2ad8050a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-284573" :question/problem "A rare species of tree was planted in a protected grove. Initially, there were $1, 200$ of these trees. The number of trees is expected to triple every $12$ years.\n\nWhich equation represents the number of trees $N(t)$, $t$ years after the trees were first planted?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85020fc6-2e58-5574-b03f-843c51009117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 36000 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222862} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbdfde0-95ce-528d-aaf0-f79672d15427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222863} {:answer/id #uuid "d632ac1e-dc99-53ff-b342-1ceb94c01172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222864} {:answer/id #uuid "3004a261-580c-56a7-9cd2-2a17bb46c388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 36000 \\cdot t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222865} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff8ad77-02a4-5b29-b638-165609758e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 36000 \\cdot 2^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222866}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a584f7-c4fb-5362-8e5c-e6a267071241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222861}] :question/id #uuid "b200d0e4-46a7-5e5f-aa37-28d8aab0331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-284864" :question/problem "A scientist observes a culture of mold with an initial count of $36, 000$ spores. The number of spores doubles every day. Which equation models the population $y$ after $t$ days have passed?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5e98502-f560-50ff-97e5-4b983406b5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222932} {:answer/id #uuid "e9604aa3-8141-5de4-a1c3-ce8fd6577a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^{t/5} + 65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222933} {:answer/id #uuid "249c0631-4dfd-5eac-ae62-60194807e309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5} + 65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222934} {:answer/id #uuid "25136907-87c9-5f2f-b61a-f843136641a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 200 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222935} {:answer/id #uuid "2b19a5c2-5331-52f2-b886-fb3c7aa09feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 200 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222936}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c7935135-4812-5427-a6a8-daa985bfff3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186222931}] :question/id #uuid "e97c736f-d17b-5ce7-b3c8-79f881e1bc7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-285212" :question/problem "In a science experiment, Priya heated a metal rod and then left it to cool in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $65^{∘}F$. At $3:00$ p.m., the rod’s temperature was $200^{∘}F$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $170^{∘}F$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $146.5^{∘}F$. The rod continued to cool over time.\n\nWhich of the following functions best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the rod $t$ minutes after it was removed from the heat?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85921e2c-036b-534e-9547-2e352b8e5861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 40)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223020} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc230a7-575b-5386-9764-94e306ffe5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223021} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ada6de-7325-5d55-bef5-9b7da6bfbbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223022} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb9da01-b19a-5887-873a-42a8ee3997d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223023} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0b2211-d8e9-5830-871a-9b735a846328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223024}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b8754688-2eee-56a9-a4bc-fafa5e18589c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223019}] :question/id #uuid "2f7e931c-0af2-5f7d-880b-54aa82e3f67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-285537" :question/problem "Let $h(x) = 8\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{x}$. What is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = h(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dca522a4-e235-5ac0-a96d-b2008a79a27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223587} {:answer/id #uuid "f844b3c2-3a1c-58e3-9fdd-ee93d0a2ace7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223588} {:answer/id #uuid "fd47cbab-e366-56c9-84cd-c9df6edb0982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186223589} {:answer/id #uuid "26e3eaa5-3cad-51a3-a135-5a7c6c1cbc7a" :answer/type {: