type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227580} {:answer/id #uuid "76db37e5-b0bf-50cb-83fc-ec1fe73c3154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227581} {:answer/id #uuid "77bc7343-8f39-591f-ab13-23d48e853178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{16}{9}\\right)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227582}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2453e2fe-950f-5c10-aeb2-e4ade0d4ebd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227577}] :question/id #uuid "7b6697c4-e57f-55ba-9c3b-ab9255978df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-325226" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $3^{-2k}4^{k}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaaca5ba-8c3f-593a-9a93-71851b5f429b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(36)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227584} {:answer/id #uuid "c2240d39-6d97-5dbf-b9d3-3892924ad402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227585} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ecdd3a-525e-5e33-9486-d78d4d7cd99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227586} {:answer/id #uuid "19b5affa-a6b9-5613-8250-c49ffb3ed8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227587} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca7ae6b-758f-5e45-ab70-32b01a2b22cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227588}] :answer-field/id #uuid "27831fc1-bde8-5190-8e47-c3f634d6b6a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227583}] :question/id #uuid "0290ad43-8cee-5a12-a565-921ef9a6160d" :question/math-academy-id "q-325227" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $3^{2k}4^{k}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2271ff-21bf-51b4-8d45-2c4647b83718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b2271ff-21bf-51b4-8d45-2c4647b83718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302533} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc5293e-1c4c-59c9-887f-e438c1d0b4b5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304194} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64825e76-f86a-56d6-92a0-e1d80a038471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64825e76-f86a-56d6-92a0-e1d80a038471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303068} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50aeb11-4c90-521c-83b1-4ae8839287ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "390843f1-e8b0-5115-a3d2-290299a8ce3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332446" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e9a1c25-344d-5dbd-be20-eff2b5d788b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e9a1c25-344d-5dbd-be20-eff2b5d788b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303243} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2d0d86-dec6-5402-9728-c6d1665a8d04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2293beab-4d00-5f11-b2f4-e3ae5bdd1a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2293beab-4d00-5f11-b2f4-e3ae5bdd1a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302674} :answer-field/id #uuid "af29e177-a4de-503a-a535-0de11fe62c50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dd8dc08-2fc9-5908-9810-58211c6eb3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332793" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(-\\frac{6}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a735c772-af97-5eea-b8e9-6a8dfb90fefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{2.3 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} + (1.6 \\cdot 10^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228268} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5e64d1-8193-5c2a-aa97-28a6bbd12e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{2.3 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r^{2}} + (9.2 \\cdot 10^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228269} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a028c9-87b3-5fdb-8ce0-e13f19c7179c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{2.3 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} - (1.6 \\cdot 10^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228270} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5266f5-54f8-5ad7-95da-336caafa30e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{1.15 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} + (1.6 \\cdot 10^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228271} {:answer/id #uuid "0eef3ee7-e041-535c-85dd-268c3df19f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{1.15 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} - (9.2 \\cdot 10^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228272}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f60acb06-e661-5395-b664-5819511bcf63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef49603-a807-5f45-982c-373a15835b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391545} {:answer/id #uuid "041f118d-162c-5545-bb0c-a3cba2bcf150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.15\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391546} {:answer/id #uuid "71fc6b2a-4a64-5b8d-aecb-dab45d68c6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391547} {:answer/id #uuid "24e292a0-859a-569e-b479-5d6a968cab39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.15\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(9.2\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391548} {:answer/id #uuid "639bf900-44e3-5ebc-86b0-d807c159046c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{{r}^{2}}+(9.2\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71fc6b2a-4a64-5b8d-aecb-dab45d68c6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391547} :answer-field/id #uuid "b31a8672-9812-5f3b-958f-3de2d774aebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575cae6c-4604-5b3a-b514-5f09e281fdca" :question/math-academy-id "q-335240" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(2.5\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=18.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(9.6\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\\right)\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(2.5\\times {10}^{8}),$\n$g=18.4,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(9.6\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 18.4\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{r}+(9.6\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 18.4\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{230\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(92.16\\times {10}^{8})-(92\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+((92.16-92)\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(0.16\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac342bc-5d31-58a3-842a-8a4e79ae42dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377193} {:answer/id #uuid "2980a552-e3d5-5564-ab0e-1bbb2358ce8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377194} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} {:answer/id #uuid "49a8983c-5113-540c-9960-edc50602eed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377196} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1780f1-fe83-536d-9909-81d9b471da59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} :answer-field/id #uuid "33da174c-337a-5186-96b2-62c3b16e65a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98f92dde-10ee-5a2e-ae0c-afe47e1981a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336451" :question/problem "A de-icing salt reservoir can hold up to $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine with $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A stronger brine with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132d6c9e-eb68-5d65-8d72-ee64a266345d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501500} {:answer/id #uuid "14f59116-2d5c-52e2-9b35-7f32ef8ca9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501506} {:answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501529} {:answer/id #uuid "df01cbb2-ff59-5cab-a987-8313d051432e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.63\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501913} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7cb287-8d09-5c98-9bf7-3d208192ac9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.99\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad053d7-10e4-578d-b606-6bda94abf500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "310f29d9-e5f9-5829-9c84-72d1324e1777" :question/math-academy-id "q-336738" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+10}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "aebdf2d3-26e8-577a-8477-2c2efb1a4064" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228377} {:answer-field/id #uuid "b4a42770-9433-54e8-b88d-ad4eac518c2e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228378}] :question/id #uuid "a8534e01-c3a5-514f-aa2a-35a95571b90c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337100" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = x\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^{2} + \\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0057fe33-994d-530f-9d6a-1d8d52f3e80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=1-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308744} {:answer/id #uuid "01633495-e9fe-50bd-a637-b4862253307f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308753} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1110b5-8718-5257-8b4b-1114bbeec5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308833} {:answer/id #uuid "e2427c75-b652-5799-8515-b39080a90533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310557} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4acb5e-4c58-59e0-96b1-5ed383eebc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01633495-e9fe-50bd-a637-b4862253307f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308753} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b701e5-888a-5c98-92a7-f2756d373e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1782a6b5-a5c5-5e52-a9f2-0cebbb762ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337103" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =-2t{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =-2t{e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =-2(t-1)+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+2-2t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=2-2t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061efbf8-2b6a-5be5-bd7b-21ad06d93409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308797} {:answer/id #uuid "21542faa-ec85-5250-b1f9-3ba21b860990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309000} {:answer/id #uuid "b45ee349-9f71-5d3a-94ee-80ec66e795cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310206} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ecaf40-a571-5685-8f52-3985bfa3e941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310481} {:answer/id #uuid "fb390867-902b-58b1-a270-d17e8f675d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "061efbf8-2b6a-5be5-bd7b-21ad06d93409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308797} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d2bf59d-6338-51c4-acd9-0887c0b311a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b007f131-5b66-5321-9c2b-80d3724de4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337105" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(1-t)^{2}}\\right)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1}=(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(t-1)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-\\frac{t-1}{t} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=\\frac{1-t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-t}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\right){t}^{2}+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},g(t)=t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68a0bc6-f30d-5411-b064-805f776486bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228392} {:answer/id #uuid "bec5c4a2-e69a-58a3-94b3-3a681c2ec258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228393} {:answer/id #uuid "3f269fd6-b9ae-5b17-b3d5-9362d69d8e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228394} {:answer/id #uuid "42cbcc13-0825-57af-be0a-c07b85f4c34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228395} {:answer/id #uuid "67173ceb-8d72-5892-b1ac-cf5cbde74ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec5c4a2-e69a-58a3-94b3-3a681c2ec258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228393} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8042cd1-bac2-5d2b-b9a4-591acf3abc28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0d9a933-a0e8-56f2-b25b-1f97723df869" :question/math-academy-id "q-337169" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4182416a97e5f0111b0823d6699f0ed39125f60289d7c7ebbb5f98d51d1a0f6.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{60}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{60}\\right)}=60q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+60q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+3{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{25},\\beta =\\frac{4}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=0.2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4dea68f2-1db4-5014-82eb-ddbb8d4d69e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228403} {:answer-field/id #uuid "61ef3c50-6638-5d76-a35c-7e1480d508b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228404}] :question/id #uuid "19be94de-0e17-54e2-85cf-71dbe2245d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337184" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = x\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^{3} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9325e5d1-fa4a-5ce0-80f1-2b1d73d21c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228440} {:answer/id #uuid "96716f40-d0f8-5848-8816-2a9ef9ed6bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228441} {:answer/id #uuid "977267b3-794b-5090-b748-b772bdf84ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\cot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228442} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e374c-61a5-55b6-af69-e44d8a4b2b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228443} {:answer/id #uuid "55f4c050-06c9-5796-a080-e7181bc8bb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228444}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81796914-d852-595b-8050-53c08ac44ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c4f0de-65d4-55a3-992e-a647b139e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365833} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c65982-0650-5e0d-996a-e78f9bb5b6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365834} {:answer/id #uuid "12d444c7-9ae8-512f-98e6-830e37b66435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365835} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ab968b-1634-5de2-9e80-f73f99b4e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365836} {:answer/id #uuid "56e33ae8-094c-5ec7-a04f-c7ad165cabe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12d444c7-9ae8-512f-98e6-830e37b66435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365835} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9008acd-b16a-5420-b119-9306ceb1ba1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9674aca-ff32-53a9-8aab-6b30c0139952" :question/math-academy-id "q-337952" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y={sec}^{3}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={sec}^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\underbrace{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f8cae8-00e4-5d66-b7ab-8dc7d80eb1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s \\ge - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228635} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6f841c-04c9-5850-890f-aa152557ae6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > 0" :d