eld-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4c533f-908a-567e-bec5-a34f0fb4c795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368304} {:answer/id #uuid "e33815ee-25fe-5ccb-b369-f0725f4674b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368305} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ad58bc-98b4-5f21-a938-56897b11dd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368306} {:answer/id #uuid "93b4c7cd-4771-5107-8cfd-b99977ef7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368307} {:answer/id #uuid "844e6b9d-a130-5b9a-a07c-6bc9ae2eab75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368308} {:answer/id #uuid "d21f58f8-5853-5eb6-84b6-008b143b0b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21f58f8-5853-5eb6-84b6-008b143b0b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368309} :answer-field/id #uuid "26c0fa7b-08b6-5bc4-a9f0-3be3ef51d6f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7da22ad8-b712-52ee-a8cd-593d4497e34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3(2+s)}}{s+2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368311} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d999a6-3413-502a-947d-25b79356a95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{s+2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368312} {:answer/id #uuid "1be7bc5f-1c6d-5d9e-a223-91b8ee41e59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3(2+s)}-1}{s+2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368313} {:answer/id #uuid "f63fe085-f93d-5fcd-bf37-289a3afe47a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63fe085-f93d-5fcd-bf37-289a3afe47a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368314} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9132969-223c-5250-b50b-37fcd34fa6cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e403a919-07cd-5914-ae97-646903c631b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-341480" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(2+s)}{[{e}^{-(2+s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e9c02f-14e9-5f32-b79c-a293214a34bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = y_{1}(x)\\ln x + x^{-1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228673} {:answer/id #uuid "d5208b18-0f8d-53aa-a1d9-e511b152b957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = b_{-1}y_{1}(x)\\ln x + x^{-1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228674} {:answer/id #uuid "4413815d-7aad-58c0-b7fa-87fe8642d713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = y_{1}(x)\\ln x + x^{3}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(3)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228675} {:answer/id #uuid "48ee413e-53fb-5a1f-8135-e79ae7b4b460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{-1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228676} {:answer/id #uuid "55c8cb48-ff13-5ae2-8cde-4c339e88fd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{3}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(3)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228677}] :answer-field/id #uuid "297f2c43-91f3-5db3-a212-9faef1b723df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5631038-02ff-5478-959b-702050aff1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379642} {:answer/id #uuid "2f485562-fee4-5334-bd58-776a6dc64a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379643} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1ae533-0b8b-5428-bafe-f583f9b82f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379644} {:answer/id #uuid "97172b7b-d96a-5752-bf3b-37b017cabfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379645} {:answer/id #uuid "7881ac04-9b8a-5136-a88c-49ff54bf4f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97172b7b-d96a-5752-bf3b-37b017cabfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379645} :answer-field/id #uuid "d405bd5b-5125-52fd-8365-832ff3fb0d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d16c3fa-9c4f-5cce-a9e5-386a0c61403b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342494" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(2x-3){y}^{′}-3y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation above is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${a}_{n}(\\lambda )$ are coefficients dependent on $\\lambda $ (solutions of the indicial equation) and determined by substitution.\nWhich of the following is the form of the second Frobenius solution ${y}_{2}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\notin Z,$ then \n${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{2}){x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2},$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\in N,$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}={\\lambda }_{2}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{7}{2}$ *is not* an integer, the other solution is the second Frobenius solution, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231320 :question/id #uuid "dc812edf-4737-575a-937b-89bc2af88c38" :question/math-academy-id "e-21963" :question/problem "If $r=\\dfrac57$, then $r^2=$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $r$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{2} &= \\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7} \\cdot \\frac{5}{7} \\\\ &= \\frac{5 \\cdot 5}{7 \\cdot 7} \\\\ &= \\frac{25}{49} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231326 :question/id #uuid "8df55194-b24b-54ad-b6cb-1ec76c833210" :question/math-academy-id "e-21970" :question/problem "The amount of paint (in liters) used to cover a wall is given by the expression $\\dfrac12w^2+\\dfrac34$, where $w$ is the width of the wall in meters. How much paint is used when the width of the wall is $\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}$?" :question/worked-solution "To determine how much paint is used when the width of the wall is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe evaluate the expression at\n\n$$\nw=\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nFirst, replacing $w$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nin the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, evaluating the resulting expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{24}{32} \\\\ &= \\frac{49}{32} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of paint used is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{32}\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb9ae0a-ccab-5851-bc46-d8fb84f0673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537298} {:answer/id #uuid "391bcc0e-c46d-58de-a271-e5921f5359be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537346} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf55ac9-9213-51d1-bb90-ad44b88e7647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537354} {:answer/id #uuid "93cc08d4-e51b-59b2-b080-974e07e72850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb9ae0a-ccab-5851-bc46-d8fb84f0673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537298} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b2f25e-b1a1-5a98-851d-8c3126524b1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c02a55f-b3d6-55fc-b2ef-8b484c0659e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343663" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508401} {:answer/id #uuid "7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508565} {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} {:answer/id #uuid "aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} :answer-field/id #uuid "820b3701-ddd6-58d9-9b7a-77aa5711efbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ccc73a6-d864-55e4-9da3-f78dd465c648" :question/math-academy-id "q-343671" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1bb9f4b-6c2e-5d6b-87d0-78e4c9a5b1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230377} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3d421-418f-598d-b619-f761de70afeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230378} {:answer/id #uuid "353a8f78-ce46-5d98-86a4-c93a4fd5aa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230379} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9ff9c8-f28b-5bbc-b56a-9dca8a667374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230380} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c51ea-ffd9-5c44-a039-8d1f95f96d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c51ea-ffd9-5c44-a039-8d1f95f96d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230381} :answer-field/id #uuid "00eff77d-799b-55ba-877d-1720b58cb296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b2630f-dea5-5b20-90c5-3365567316a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344172" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d8f6711f76fa9e55f11087c99d0e626ebc665e0f44beeefe3f67e9d35159618.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{9}{50}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{6}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233030 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62c0b89d-1d8a-5723-8092-97486825267c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100459" :question/problem "If ${216}^{3a+4}={\\left(\\frac{1}{36}\\right)}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={\\left(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}}\\right)}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233051 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d746064f-9bc3-5f26-9c93-afaf01c3e7e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-101719" :question/problem "Given that $y=7-4{log}_{3}x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-4{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =0-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} & ={\\left(-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\right)\\,|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10447d27-bbad-5243-827a-0ec88f52db0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512131} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8fd27-a6bb-5d05-8c7a-556b2db63a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512359} {:answer/id #uuid "71a944ae-ff58-5447-8134-5585f04d64b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512370} {:answer/id #uuid "b98cc99c-1897-5580-9a45-71a60760d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512546} {:answer/id #uuid "d6da7332-d0ae-5c57-a840-c9de6058e7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db8fd27-a6bb-5d05-8c7a-556b2db63a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512359} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf2afe7-a005-5071-84b9-bd40024ce506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d862336-2821-5236-aa0c-5f338ff1395d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102613" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=3$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233077 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cba81123-55c3-55a5-9eb0-35c472e805bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103257" :question/problem "Consider the sequence starting from ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}$  with the property $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{3}{4}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$ Write the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence using sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the sequence is a geometric sequence since the ratio of any two successive terms is constant.\n\nThe first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5},$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term ${a}_{n}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{-1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right){\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233087 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cc9778d-34a0-53fe-a02e-327d24fefea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104437" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cot}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the derivative using the product rule:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+x(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x-x{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{\\pi /4} & =\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{csc}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c08502-d0d5-5e72-af2d-a3ef7cdf0292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241675} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf2067a-14ff-5a00-adc0-d41c69dcd714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241861} {:answer/id #uuid "659f5ff8-46d6-55a7-a779-874f201a7a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242093} {:answer/id #uuid "f5df124e-f5dc-548a-9350-06239c8666e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243353} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc7784a-720a-585a-a0f1-5b62e36cba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffc7784a-720a-585a-a0f1-5b62e36cba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243432} :answer-field/id #uuid "84916584-efb6-58b6-a99a-7f67a30e54da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c02d420-5776-5e40-b2a8-716b6f6c3925" :question/math-academy-id "q-107499" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233107 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8dfbbbdb-5ea1-54bb-aa69-852fdb1bfb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-107659" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+…-\\frac{41}{4}-12\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)\\left(-\\frac{7}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{7}{4}n+\\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{15}{4}+12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{63}{4} \\\\ n & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=9.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233113 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49708de8-941e-50f5-938e-ceca5ba94528" :question/math-academy-id "q-107794" :question/problem "Calculate the sum of the first $15$ terms of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n-1+\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}+⋯\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=15$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=-1.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=\\frac{3}{5}$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2(-1)+(15-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-2+8.4) \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 6.4 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233116 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3220f14-15f6-53f4-bf67-85844b3d99e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108505" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)=8{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}) \\\\ [({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}(y))] & =24{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =24{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y))}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24y-\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233118 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09438de3-169c-5930-a51b-e7ea865bc196" :question/math-academy-id "q-108681" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{3}\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\right)}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233130 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fbae22f-6bd7-5d9b-9385-2d221b17f558" :question/math-academy-id "q-110212" :question/problem "The geometric series $2\\,401+343+⋯+7$  can be expressed using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2\\,401.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{343}{2\\,401}=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 7 & =2\\,401\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2\\,401} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{343} & =\\frac{1}{{7}^{N-1}} \\\\ 343 & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ {7}^{3} & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ 3 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,401+343+⋯+7 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1049ad2b-d3c5-598f-a813-b594700c836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478580} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3ce2a3-15e3-5f28-8cd5-6e81df3d84f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478625} {:answer/id #uuid "84826b57-c122-587c-baf5-141a650be923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478762} {:answer/id #uuid "85d2c120-3987-58fb-b1a8-1cdfdf73f75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478765} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeb8da5-4868-5726-bf1c-39c8f81f6da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84826b57-c122-587c-baf5-141a650be923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478762} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d842570-384d-5f9a-8934-5fc8f5ec2231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad2b776-429a-5d87-afc3-be9a913d2b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110328" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)}} \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233136 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e208b3-c758-5793-bf13-95c2af152c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110540" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$  for $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}2x}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arcsine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}2x}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} {:answer/id #uuid "62de7813-7886-5bed-86cd-1ca23e600b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21{x}^{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513799} {:answer/id #uuid "65428549-ccd7-5201-8026-265c549f0352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513804} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af8b6-1444-5218-bbb1-bf6a887d157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{19}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513863} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf69104-089b-5eec-8f81-3bf53d5632ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{x}^{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} :answer-field/id #uuid "6982cbcb-0bd9-554d-8104-e02dd398a383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113665" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} \\\\ &= {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233175 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7949e7bf-09d1-5ce9-ac3c-0d7f26b2e6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113670" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} \\\\ &= {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233176 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353f4779-4251-5a93-b99f-1eda6757b24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113674" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
